# Docker build-context exclusions (phase-1 task-05).
#
# Both infra/Dockerfile.api and infra/Dockerfile.web build with the REPO
# ROOT as context (`build: {context: .., dockerfile: infra/Dockerfile.*}`
# in infra/docker-compose.yml) — without this file, every `docker build`
# would tar up the multi-gigabyte reference_project/ vendor tree, every
# node_modules/.venv, and language caches before the daemon even starts
# resolving layers. None of these are COPYed by either Dockerfile, so
# excluding them changes build context size only, not image contents.

# vendored reference project — never part of any image
reference_project/

# VCS / editor / local tool state
.git/
.claude/
.superpowers/

# node / pnpm
node_modules/
**/node_modules/
.next/
**/.next/

# python
.venv/
**/.venv/
__pycache__/
**/__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
**/.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
**/.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
**/.ruff_cache/
.import_linter_cache/
**/.import_linter_cache/

# coverage / build artifacts
coverage/
**/coverage/
*.tsbuildinfo

# secrets — **/-anchored (verified live) so nested app envs (apps/admin/.env,
# apps/admin/.env.local, etc.) are excluded too, not just the build-context
# root: the previous un-anchored `.env` / `*.env` pair only matched at the
# context root, so a `docker build` from repo root would COPY nested env
# files straight into web images. .dockerignore patterns are evaluated
# client-side before the context tar is sent to the daemon, so a correctly
# anchored pattern really does keep matches off the wire. The negation below
# is deliberately root-anchored (no `**/` prefix) — verified live that Docker
# honors that as "only the context-root .env.example", which is exactly the
# one tracked file this needs to re-allow; a `!**/.env.example` form was also
# tried and rejected because it reaches inside otherwise-excluded directories
# (e.g. it resurrected reference_project/**/.env.example files).
**/.env*
!.env.example

# docs — not needed inside any image
docs/
*.md
